221: \section{Architecture}
222: 
223: The workflow separates source work from publication work, and the separation is
224: the organizing contract for the rest of the supplement.  BEDC source files are
225: treated as finite-kernel theory together with proof obligations and separate
226: implementation contracts.  Lean and related markers are formal-verification
227: records for named obligations, not object-language closure by themselves.
228: Finite manifests, evaluator rows, bundled snapshots, and command logs are the
229: substrate witnesses from which publication certificates are built.  AI agents
230: remain advisory: they may propose, review, and triage, but they do not promote
231: claims into accepted theory.  The automath layer is therefore a publication
232: compiler: it packages the source-theory interface, finite witnesses, route state,
233: and submission boundary without merging those evidence axes.
234: 
235: \begin{definition}[Architecture-axis map]
236: \label{def:architecture-axis-map}
237: The compiler architecture uses five disjoint evidence axes.  An accepted claim may
238: read evidence from an axis only at the admissible evidence level shown in
239: the displayed axis map; the negative boundary in the last column
240: is part of the claim certificate and cannot be discharged by prose from another
241: axis.
242: \begin{center}
243: \small
244: \begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{p{.16\textwidth}p{.27\textwidth}p{.27\textwidth}X}
245: \toprule
246: Axis & Role in this paper & Admissible evidence & Negative boundary \\
247: \midrule
248: BEDC source theory & finite-kernel theory, proof obligations, and implementation
249: contracts & pinned source paths, commits, imported labels, bounded summaries, and
250: source-interface records & not a publication acceptance record, not a fresh rebuild,
251: and not a complete BEDC exposition \\
252: Lean markers & formal-verification records for named obligations & recorded build,
253: axiom-purity, marker-existence, or source-interface rows when named by the package &
254: not object-language closure, not discovery by itself, and not proof of unrelated
255: claims \\
256: Finite witnesses & manifest/evaluator substrate evidence for artifacts and package
257: certificates & manifest rows, evaluator outcomes, bundled snapshots, verifier logs,
258: file digests, and limitation ledgers & not semantic universality, not whole-tree
259: dynamic validation, and not an unbounded artifact rerun \\
260: Agents & proposal, search, review, ranking, and repair assistance & advisory drafts,
261: review reports, scheduler scores, and gate-input suggestions after being recorded
262: as such & not proof evidence, not human promotion, not novelty certification, and
263: not gate passage \\
264: Automath publication compiler & intake, route, overlap, submission-pack, and support
265: surface governance & promotion records, active-paper markers, overlap ledgers,
266: submission-interface maps, digest manifests, command logs, and upload-or-archive
267: records when present & not the whole mathematical contribution, not venue
268: acceptance, and not authority to merge evidence axes \\
269: \bottomrule
270: \end{tabularx}
271: \end{center}
272: \end{definition}
273: 
274: \begin{proposition}[Axis non-escalation]
275: \label{prop:architecture-axis-non-escalation}
276: In any record-gate-sound package using Definition~\ref{def:architecture-axis-map},
277: passage or evidence on one architecture axis cannot certify a load-bearing claim
278: whose claim kind requires another axis.  More precisely, if a claim certificate
279: uses an axis $A$ but its stronger reading needs an evidence item or negative-boundary
280: discharge listed only on a different axis $A'$, then the stronger reading is not
281: accepted unless the package contains a separate submitted coordinate for $A'$ and
282: the gates assigned to that coordinate pass.  Consequently
283: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} constructs only finite publication
284: certificates whose axis coordinates are explicitly present in the submitted support
285: surface; it does not infer BEDC closure from Lean markers, artifact semantics from
286: manifest rows, proof evidence from agents, venue upload from automath route state,
287: or support-surface availability from local bundle paths.
288: \end{proposition}
289: 
290: \begin{proof}
291: The operational interface records a claim by finite coordinates: claim kind,
292: evidence surface, evidence level, pass records assigned by $\Gamma$, and the
293: boundary field.  Definition~\ref{def:architecture-axis-map} makes the axis of each
294: such coordinate explicit and declares the corresponding negative boundary.  If a
295: stronger reading needs an item on a different axis $A'$, then either the claim-kind
296: coordinate does not name the kind requiring $A'$, the evidence surface does not
297: contain an admissible $A'$ item, a required $A'$ pass record is missing, or the
298: boundary field still excludes the stronger reading.  In all four cases the finite
299: record verifier of Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier}
300: rejects the stronger reading: the dispatch table cannot assign an unmentioned gate,
301: the surface check cannot read an unsubmitted path or log, the pass-record check
302: cannot be satisfied without the row, and the boundary check preserves the declared
303: negative boundary.
304: 
305: Conversely, when a separate coordinate for $A'$ is submitted, it is just another
306: finite component of the package.  It can be accepted only by the same schema:
307: its claim kind must dispatch to the appropriate gates, its evidence surface must
308: list the admissible $A'$ evidence, and its boundary must state exactly what is and
309: is not certified.  Therefore evidence never moves between axes by paraphrase or by
310: agent-generated text; it moves only by adding an explicit finite coordinate and
311: passing the assigned gates.  Applying this to the five rows of
312: Definition~\ref{def:architecture-axis-map} gives the listed consequences.  They are
313: also the axis-level instance of the coordinate-determination result later isolated
314: in Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}: facts outside the
315: finite coordinates of the accepted package are not conclusions of the certificate.
316: \end{proof}
317: The reader path is therefore architectural before it is logistical.  The rest of
318: the supplement first fixes the source-interface spine, then defines the finite
319: record verifier, then proves the certificate construction and non-escalation
320: boundary, then instantiates that boundary on the current six-row package, and
321: only then lists the local case snapshots and command-run inventory.  File tables
322: are included as finite witnesses for this interface, not as a substitute for the
323: compiler architecture.
324: 
325: Concretely, the newmath source workspace contains Lean files, scripts, and
326: artifact sources.  The automath publication workspace contains intake packets,
327: active paper tracks, review state, and submission metadata.  A candidate first
328: enters an intake seed.  It becomes an active paper only after a human promotion
329: decision creates a \texttt{2026\_*} directory with \texttt{main.tex} and
330: \texttt{PIPELINE.md}.  This prevents source organization from being mistaken for
331: a submission route.
332: 
333: The source side supplies a discovery-gate calculus.  In this calculus,
334: statement-only and code-only candidates are insufficient; mechanical expansion
335: and compression are not discovery by themselves; discovery claims require a
336: semantic certificate and a ledger witness; demotion preserves source-interface
337: audit soundness;
338: accepted claims must form an auditable lineage DAG; and a pipeline is safe only
339: when discovery claims pass the gate before being recorded as accepted work.
340: The reviewer-facing source snapshot is
341: \path{review_bundle/source_snapshots/automated_theory_discovery_pipeline_calculus_3fb3d6a0641767388a401883062aa522ea0b397b.tex},
342: copied from the newmath repository path
343: \path{papers/bedc/parts/visions/audit_map_methodology/automated_theory_discovery_pipeline_calculus.tex}
344: at source commit \path{3fb3d6a0641767388a401883062aa522ea0b397b}
345: \cite{bedc-newmath-source}.  The imported interface labels are listed in the
346: following source-binding record:
