434: \begin{lemma}[Manual bounded source-interface extraction correctness]
435: \label{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness}
436: For the pinned newmath repository path
437: \path{papers/bedc/parts/visions/audit_map_methodology/automated_theory_discovery_pipeline_calculus.tex}
438: at commit \path{3fb3d6a0641767388a401883062aa522ea0b397b},
439: Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record} is the complete imported interface used
440: by this paper at the manual path-verified level and only at that level.  Its extraction certificate is:
441: manual line-range audit against the pinned file, mirrored in
442: \path{source_interface_record.json}, extraction date 2026-06-01, and summary
443: mode ``bounded paraphrase'' rather than replacement of the full source
444: statements.  Consequently every use below of an imported discovery-gate label is
445: limited to the corresponding row's path, commit, label, line range, and bounded
446: summary.  Even when an explicitly named executable finite record comparison is
447: also invoked, the upgraded content is only equality and containment of those
448: finite fields; exact source statements are not imported.  In particular, the
449: lemma is not a fresh Lean/BEDC rebuild, not an axiom-purity certificate, and not
450: a semantic proof of the imported source theorem statements.
451: \end{lemma}
452: 
453: \begin{proof}
454: The first two columns of Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record} give, for every
455: imported label named in the source-binding record, the source label and the line
456: range from which the interface is extracted.  The paragraph immediately before
457: the table fixes the only source file and commit used for those labels; the
458: paragraph immediately after the table fixes the extraction date and identifies
459: \path{source_interface_record.json} as the mirrored check record.  The third
460: column deliberately records bounded summaries: each row states only the
461: interface property needed later, such as insufficiency of statement-only records,
462: certificate-and-ledger requirements, demotion soundness, lineage acyclicity,
463: bounded classifier-change selection, or pipeline safety.  No later theorem cites
464: an imported label outside this sixteen-row list, and no later proof uses a
465: stronger form than the corresponding bounded summary.  A Lean rebuild,
466: axiom-purity check, exact source-statement import, or semantic source-theorem
467: validation would require additional command-result or formal-source coordinates;
468: none is among the fields read in this lemma.  Thus the table is complete for the
469: imported interface of this paper and correct at the stated evidence level: exact
470: as to path, commit, label, line range, method, date, and summary mode, and
471: bounded as to mathematical restatement.\end{proof}
472: 
473: \begin{proposition}[Manual source-interface non-escalation]
474: \label{prop:manual-source-interface-non-escalation}
475: Let $I$ be the imported discovery-gate interface certified by
476: Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness}.  Any later use of an
477: imported source label in this paper is a consequence of $I$ only if it is a
478: function of the finite fields recorded in Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record}:
479: the source path, pinned commit, label, line range, extraction date, bounded
480: summary, and evidence-level qualifier.  A proposed stronger use---for example an
481: exact import of the original source theorem statement, a fresh BEDC or Lean
482: rebuild, an axiom-purity statement, or a semantic validation of the imported
483: source theorem---is accepted by the finite-record safety interface only after the
484: submitted record surface is enlarged by the corresponding source-statement,
485: formal-build, or command-run coordinate and by the assigned pass records for that
486: coordinate.  Without that enlargement the stronger use is rejected as a finite
487: bad subrecord and the accepted consequence remains the manual path-verified
488: bounded interface.
489: \end{proposition}
490: 
491: \begin{proof}
492: Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness} identifies exactly the
493: finite fields in $I$ and states that the summaries are bounded paraphrases rather
494: than replacements of the original source statements.  Therefore two possible
495: review states that agree on those fields but differ in an unsubmitted source
496: checkout, proof-assistant state, axiom audit, or executable extraction log give
497: the same values to every coordinate read by $I$.  By
498: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, no claim depending on
499: one of the differing unsubmitted coordinates is a consequence of the finite
500: interface record.
501: 
502: If a stronger use is made load-bearing, then it strictly enlarges the original
503: source-interface reading in the stronger-reading preorder of
504: Definition~\ref{def:stronger-reading-preorder}: exact source-statement import
505: adds an evidence-surface coordinate, a BEDC or Lean rebuild adds formal-source or
506: command-run coordinates, axiom-purity adds an assigned gate and pass record, and
507: semantic validation adds a new claim-kind or boundary coordinate.  The normal
508: form of Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form} says that such a
509: genuine strengthening is accepted exactly when the added finite coordinates and
510: their gate records are supplied.  If they are absent,
511: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} returns the missing
512: coordinate as a finite bad subrecord.  Thus the source import used downstream,
513: including in Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}, remains the manual
514: path-verified bounded interface and nothing stronger.\end{proof}
515: 
